              "text": "There are several safe, secure, and official ways to pay your federal taxes online. The best method depends on whether you want to pay directly from your bank account (free) or with a credit/debit card (fees apply).\n\n---\n\n### 1. Directly from a Bank Account (Free)\nPaying directly from your checking or savings account is the most popular option because it is **100% free** and provides instant confirmation.\n\n*   **IRS Direct Pay:** This is the easiest tool for individuals to pay federal income tax, estimated taxes, or extensions. \n    *   **How it works:** You do not need to register or create an account. You verify your identity by entering personal information from a previous year's tax return.\n    *   **Where to go:** Visit [IRS Direct Pay](https://www.irs.gov/payments/direct-pay).\n*   **Your IRS Online Account:** If you want to see exactly how much you owe first, you can log into your personal IRS account.\n    *   **How it works:** You log in securely (typically using ID.me). From there, you can view your balance, payment history, tax records, and schedule a payment. \n    *   **Where to go:** Go to [IRS Online Account](https://www.irs.gov/your-online-account).\n\n---\n\n### 2. By Debit Card, Credit Card, or Digital Wallet (Fees Apply)\nYou can pay online with a card or digital wallet (like PayPal), but **third-party processing fees will apply**. The IRS itself does not keep these fees. \n\nThe IRS utilizes two primary approved payment processors: **Pay1040** and **ACI Payments, Inc.** (Note: *PayUSAtax is no longer an active partner*).\n\n*   **Debit Cards:** \n    *   **Fees:** Typically a flat fee of **$2.10 to $2.15** per transaction.\n*   **Credit Cards:** \n    *   **Fees:** Usually **1.75% to 1.85%** of the total payment amount (with a minimum charge of $2.50). \n    *   *Tip:* Paying with a credit card can be useful if you are trying to earn credit card points or welcome bonuses, but ensure the value of the rewards outweighs the processing fee.\n*   **Where to go:** You can access both processors directly through the [IRS Pay by Card](https://www.irs.gov/payments/pay-your-taxes-by-debit-or-credit-card) page.\n\n---\n\n### 3. Electronic Funds Withdrawal (EFW)\nIf you are currently e-filing your tax return using tax software (such as TurboTax, H&R Block, TaxSlayer, or FreeTaxUSA), you can submit your payment details directly during the filing process. \n*   You simply enter your routing and bank account numbers, choose the date you want the funds to be withdrawn, and submit it with your return.\n\n---\n\n### 4. For Businesses: EFTPS (Electronic Federal Tax Payment System)\nIf you are a business owner or need to make large federal tax deposits (or high-volume estimated tax payments):\n*   **EFTPS** is a free system that allows you to schedule payments up to 365 days in advance.\n*   *Note:* It requires an enrollment process, and you will be mailed a PIN to activate your account before you can use it. \n*   **Where to go:** Visit [EFTPS.gov](https://www.eftps.gov). *(Note: The IRS sunsetted new individual registrations for EFTPS in late 2025, but it remains fully operational for business tax payments).*\n\n---\n\n### What if you can't pay the full amount?\nIf you cannot pay your tax bill in full, you should still file your return on time to avoid late-filing penalties. You can then apply online for an IRS Payment Plan (Installment Agreement):\n*   **Short-term payment plan:** Up to 180 days to pay (minimal setup fees).\n*   **Long-term installment agreement:** Monthly payments for up to 72 months (requires a setup fee, though fees are reduced if you set up direct debit).\n*   You can apply directly via the [IRS Online Payment Agreement](https://www.irs.gov/payments/online-payment-agreement-application) tool.\n\n---\n\n### \u26a0\ufe0f Security Warning\nAlways make sure you are on an official government website before entering your bank account or Social Security details. Official federal websites always end in **`.gov`** (such as `IRS.gov` or `EFTPS.gov`). The IRS will **never** text you or email you out of the blue asking for immediate online payments.",
